WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:03.300
Hi everyone, my name is Sai Vennam with the IBM Cloud team.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.460
Today we want to talk about Managed Kubernetes.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.640
Kubernetes is an open source platform

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.760
allowing you to run your containerized workloads.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.520
Manage Kubernetes on the other hand

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.260
is a service provided by cloud providers

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.600
and comes with a number of advantages.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:19.320
For one,

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.260
it's very easy to spin up new Kubernetes clusters from scratch

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.919
and add additional computing power in response to load.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:27.800
In addition,

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.300
it allows you to integrate with cutting-edge open source capabilities,

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.400
as well as familiar cloud provider services.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.840
Finally, you get peace of mind with security across the board.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.520
Let's go through an example of spinning up a Kubernetes cluster

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.820
on IBM Cloud Kubernetes Service.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.260
First we'll choose the name for the cluster,

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.200
as well as the region that we want to spin it up in.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.960
We can drill in deeper and choose the exact datacenter

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.600
that we want our cluster to live in.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.860
In addition, we can also choose a multi-zone cluster.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.540
This allows us to spread our Kubernetes cluster

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.940
across multiple datacenters in the same region.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.460
This gives us high availability

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:07.500
if anything went wrong with one of the data centers.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.720
Next, we can choose the flavor for the computing power

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.360
for this Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.580
We can stick with virtual computing, or even bare metal.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:20.060
Bare metal allows you to get dedicated access to the entire machine,

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:23.780
allowing you to avoid that "noisy neighbor" issue.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.340
Next you can even define GPUs for your compute power,

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:29.680
in case you had something like machine learning,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.480
or image or video processing

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.060
that can take advantage of GPUs.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.140
Finally, scrolling down to the bottom,

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.840
we can choose a number of worker nodes that we want to spin up,

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.160
and go ahead and create that cluster.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.680
In a few minutes, your Kubernetes cluster will be provisioned

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.000
and ready to use.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.260
Kubernetes is an open source platform

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.100
and is a part of the Cloud Native Computing Foundation.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.400
To ensure portability of your workloads across any Kubernetes Service Provider,

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.420
the CNCF provides conformance testing.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.920
This enables you to avoid vendor lock-in

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.560
and even allows you to do multi-cloud deployments

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.649
of your Kubernetes based applications.

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:07.340
Here at IBM we're strong proponents of open standards,

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:10.000
as well as projects in the open source community.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.880
These include those in the CNCF,

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.660
as well as projects like Istio and Knative.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.140
In fact, you can try managed Istio and managed Knative today

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:20.640
on IBM Cloud Kubernetes Service.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.720
Outside of those managed offerings,

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.087
we also offer tutorials and documentation

00:02:25.087 --> 00:02:27.880
to integrate with cutting edge open source capabilities,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.960
as well as IBM tooling and offerings.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.320
Finally, let's talk about security.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:34.960
Security is something that should be integrated

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.660
into every stage of your DevOps work flow.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:39.640
With IBM Cloud Kubernetes Service

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.600
we'll make sure you're protected every step of the way.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.420
First, we have trusted private registries

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.240
that scan your images for any vulnerabilities.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.220
Second, we will make sure that those images are scanned

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.400
as they're being pushed into a  Kubernetes cluster.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:55.600
Behind the scenes,

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.080
we're always making sure that

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.380
any vulnerabilities in the technology stack that powers your cluster

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.540
are caught right away.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:06.060
Thanks for watching this quick overview of Manage Kubernetes Services.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.120
If you like this video or have any questions

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:09.800
be sure to drop a comment below.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.100
Stay tuned for more videos like this in the future

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.540
and be sure to like and subscribe.

